; lib/context.ssa: the current context (fm3 question 71). `context()` is
; a pointer to it and `context_set(p)` makes `p` the current one; a
; function is never handed it. What it points at is the program's
; business: a struct of state, read and written by `load` and `store`
; through the pointer. The bodies here are the meaning, one word of
; the program's data. A platform that keeps the pointer in a register
; says so by a rule (`ext context`: x28 on arm64, s11 on riscv64, a
; global on wasm32), and there a read of it is no instruction where
; nothing could have changed it (ssa.md, *The current context*). Under
; the JIT the word below is also where the context waits between two
; calls from the host, whose register it is outside them.

data __context: array(u64, 1)

fn context() -> ptr
    h: ptr = addr __context
    c: ptr = load h
    ret c

fn context_set(c: ptr)
    h: ptr = addr __context
    store c, h
    ret
